# Host Taskmaster turns, every scheme's and the Overlord's, and the shared Citadel tools pool every host turn calls
# (src/turn-launch.ts TASKMASTER_SLICE, about 70M), under citadel.slice
# and apart from citadel-turns.slice (#1280). Cara, 2026-11-20: "when we do have issues they become really hard to debug
# and fix if the taskmaster also becomes unresponsive". On 2026-11-20 minion turns plus a land filled the turns'
# 10G and stalled every turn in it, the Taskmasters too; here a minion storm in that slice doesn't throttle them. A
# storm across all of citadel.slice (its 19G) still reaches them, and the kernel's own oom-killer can still pick them.
# Sized from the journal's "memory peak" lines, 2026-11-18 to 20: 68 Taskmaster turns, p50 404M, p90 463M, max 572M.
# MemoryHigh 4G holds about seven at their largest at once and keeps one runaway from crowding the server.
# CPUWeight 400 against 100 for minion turns and containers (the server has 1000): at load 70 even hooks timed out.
# ManagedOOMPreference=omit, as the server has (#1257): when oomd acts on user@.service's pressure it passes them over
# for a minion turn. Each unit sets it too. Uninstalled, the slice still keeps them apart, with no limits of its own.
# No MemoryLow yet. A reserve here does protect against reclaim that citadel.slice's MemoryHigh starts, without root
# (measured for citadel-turns.slice's MemoryLow=8G, #1338), but not against reclaim the whole box starts, since
# user.slice above has none. systemd-oomd never kills for this slice's own pressure (its drop-in, deploy/oomd.md).
[Unit]
Description=Taskmaster turns of Citadel

[Slice]
MemoryHigh=4G
CPUWeight=400
ManagedOOMPreference=omit
